          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit",
          "year": 2018,
          "date_decided": "2018-10-18",
          "holding": "Texas long-term foster children have a substantive due process right to be free from an unreasonable risk of harm, and the State's caseworker-caseload and monitoring failures supported class-wide liability, although the district court's injunction was significantly overbroad.",
          "relevance": "The controlling systemic authority for Texas: it is the Fifth Circuit's framework for caseworker caseloads, placement oversight and monitoring of children in DFPS conservatorship, and — because Suter forecloses private Title IV-E enforcement — it is the mechanism that has actually produced federal oversight of this node in Texas.",
          "quote": "we hold that the General Class and the LFC subclass were properly certified",
